  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 PM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES WV imagery indicates a broad troughing pattern \nacross central and eastern Canada and ridging centered over the Four \nCorners, while the Great Lakes region remains under largely zonal \nflow. Skies will be mostly clear this afternoon before partly cloudy \nconditions take over this evening, with potential for some patchy \nfog overnight through early Tuesday morning. Low temperatures \ntonight are expected to be in the upper 50s along the shoreline, \ndipping into the low 50s inland. \n\nBroad, low-amplitude troughing will continue to exist over the Great \nLakes into the Northeast on Tuesday and Wednesday along the southern \nperiphery of the Hudson Bay low. Lower level flow is ill-defined, \nbut a weak zonally-orented convergence boundary is forecast to \nevolve on Tuesday, roughly bisecting the UP and possibly reinforced \nby the Superior lake breeze. There is also some weak positive theta-\ne advection in the 925-850 mb layer advertised by the NAM. Models \ndiffer substantially on the degree of instability present, ranging \nfrom near zero on the HRRR to near 1500 J/kg on the NAM3km. These \ndifferences are tied to the degree of near-surface moisture, as \nmidlevel lapse rates remain fairly modest. Ensemble mean approach, \nas per the 12Z HREF, suggests SBCAPE values remain below 1000 J/kg, \nwhich will keep the likelihood of any stronger storms low, despite \nample deep layer shear of around 40 kt. CAMs differ in shower \ncoverage as well, and overall have trended lower likely due to the \nweak forcing and questionable low-level moisture. This keeps \ncoverage generally in the 20-40% range, highest in the \nafternoon/evening. \n\nThe pattern does not change much into Wednesday, and questions about \nprecipitation coverage continue accordingly. This will hinge upon \nmoisture availability both near the surface and at upper levels. \nPoPs range from near zero over the west to near 50% over the south-\ncentral where there is the best probability of better moisture \npooling ahead of a weak boundary approaching from the west. Although \nisolated storms cannot be ruled out, severe weather is again not \nexpected due to the lack of instability.\n\nTemperatures will be seasonable through the week, but with a slight \ndownward trend toward the end of the week into next weekend. A drier \nairmass will arrive for Thursday and Friday keeping those days dry \nas ridging builds upstream, putting the local area in an ideal \nlocation for subsidence.",
